# @DISTRO_NAME@

@DISTRO_NAME@ is a Linux distribution for desktop and laptop PCs: Ubuntu @BASE_VERSION@ underneath, and on top the desktop of [Omarchy](https://omarchy.org), a keyboard-driven desktop known for its themes and its tiling windows, which Omarchy itself ships only on Arch Linux. @DISTRO_NAME@ brings that desktop to Ubuntu, so you get Omarchy's look and workflow with Ubuntu's software, updates and hardware support.

## Why install @DISTRO_NAME@

**Software made for Ubuntu works.** @DISTRO_NAME@ is Ubuntu @BASE_VERSION@: the same kernel, drivers and security updates, from Ubuntu's own archive, installed and updated with apt. Software that vendors publish for Ubuntu installs the same way as on Ubuntu, from the vendors' own packages and repositories; the menu's Install entries set up Chrome, VS Code, 1Password, Spotify, Signal, Tailscale and others that way. The Snap Store and Flathub are both ready to use from the start, so applications that come as snaps or Flatpaks install without any setup.

**Secure Boot can stay on.** @DISTRO_NAME@ starts with Secure Boot on or off, through the same chain of signed programs Ubuntu uses, and never asks you to turn Secure Boot off or to enroll a key of your own. NVIDIA graphics work with Secure Boot on too: the installer picks NVIDIA's open driver with modules Canonical signed, so the driver loads with Secure Boot on, and the USB stick carries the driver, so installing the driver needs no network.

**Your data is protected and recoverable.** The installer encrypts the disk by default. With the installer's default file system, btrfs, @DISTRO_NAME@ also takes a snapshot of the system with Timeshift before every update, so an update that goes wrong can be rolled back, and when the computer changes hands, Setup > Reset Computer returns the computer to a fresh install that asks the next owner to set up an account.

**The Omarchy desktop, with Omarchy's plugins and themes.** Hyprland's tiling windows, Omarchy's bar, menus, key bindings and themes, and the extras people share for Omarchy: the plugins in Omarchy's marketplace (<https://plugins.omarchy.org>) and the themes on Omarchy's theme list (<https://omarchy.org/themes/>) are added from the menu and work on @DISTRO_NAME@, except plugins that need software only Arch Linux provides.

**Laptops with two GPUs are handled.** On a laptop with both built-in graphics and an NVIDIA or AMD graphics card, the desktop and ordinary applications run on the built-in graphics, and the graphics card is used by the programs that ask for it, such as games started from Steam. AMD graphics cards have not been tried on real hardware yet.

## Apple hardware

@DISTRO_NAME@ has not been tried on a Mac yet. The Mac support below is Omarchy's, adapted to Ubuntu, and the installer applies each Mac fix only on the models it is for.

- **Apple Studio Display:** brightness works, from the keyboard's brightness keys and the desktop's controls. Linux has no standard way to set the Studio Display's brightness; @DISTRO_NAME@ includes a program that sets the brightness over the display's USB connection.
- **MacBooks from 2012 and early 2013:** the installer sets up the Wi-Fi chip (Broadcom BCM4331), which needs a network connection during installation to download the chip's firmware.
- **Macs from 2015 on with Broadcom Wi-Fi:** Wi-Fi connects to networks that accept both WPA2 and WPA3. On these Macs the Wi-Fi chip's own firmware fails the connection handshake with such networks, which looks like a rejected password, so @DISTRO_NAME@ has the system perform the handshake instead.
- **The 12-inch MacBook (2015 to 2017) and the 2016 and 2017 MacBook Pros:** the keyboard, which connects over SPI rather than USB, works at the disk encryption prompt during startup, and the built-in SSD wakes up after sleep.
- **Not supported:** Macs with Apple's T2 chip (most Macs from 2018 to 2020), Macs with Apple silicon (M1 and later), and the Broadcom BCM4360 Wi-Fi of 2013 to 2015 MacBooks, for which @DISTRO_NAME@ installs no driver.

## What @DISTRO_NAME@ runs on

- A 64-bit Intel or AMD PC that starts in UEFI mode. Computers that start only in the older BIOS mode are not supported; on a computer whose firmware offers both modes, @DISTRO_NAME@ starts when UEFI mode is selected.
- At least 4 GB of memory and 20 GB of disk space.
- The first computer @DISTRO_NAME@ is made for, and the one @DISTRO_NAME@ is tested on, is a Lenovo Legion Pro 7 laptop with Intel and NVIDIA graphics. The fixes Omarchy carries for other laptops (ASUS, Dell XPS, Framework, Microsoft Surface, MacBooks) are applied at installation on the models they are for; most of them have not been tried on those models yet.

## Getting @DISTRO_NAME@

@DISTRO_NAME@ is new, and there is no download yet: the USB installer is built from this repository's source, as [docs/README.md, "Building the distro from source"](docs/README.md#building-the-distro-from-source) describes. Once installed, @DISTRO_NAME@ updates from its own package repository and Ubuntu's.

## License

@DISTRO_NAME@'s own files, the build scripts, configuration, packaging and documentation in this repository, are under the MIT license, copyright 2026 santisbon: see [LICENSE](LICENSE). The software @DISTRO_NAME@ installs keeps its own licenses. Ubuntu's packages carry theirs, and each package built in this repository lists its licenses, file by file, in its `debian/copyright`: the Omarchy desktop is MIT, like Omarchy itself, and the third-party programs @DISTRO_NAME@ packages keep their upstream licenses, such as GPL-2+ for `asdcontrol`, LGPL-3.0 for Quickshell and the SIL Open Font License for the fonts. The license covers the code, not the name @DISTRO_NAME@.

